%0 Conference Paper
%T TONIC: towards oblivious neural inference compiler
%@ 9781450381048
%U https://doi.org/10.1145/3412841.3441929
%R 10.1145/3412841.3441929
%X Privacy-preserving deep learning computing becomes popular these days as it helps protect, for example, both user data and deep neural network (DNN) model parameters at the same time with cryptographic techniques. In particular, significant efforts have been made to leverage secure two-party computation schemes for preventing user/model data from disclosure during DNN inference. Nevertheless, the existing works require manual intervention while converting trained models into secure computation programs, which is not scalable to modern deep networks efficiently. In this work, we propose a compiler framework, TONIC, to do the conversion automatically with scalability. Given a pre-trained DNN model, TONIC converts it into one of two secure two-party computation languages, i.e., ObliVM and ABY. Based on tailored backends built on top of a DNN compiler, TVM, our case studies show that TONIC is able to automatically convert popular DNN models, such as CryptoNets and MobileNetV2, into the corresponding programs for secure computations.
%B Proceedings of the 36th Annual ACM Symposium on Applied Computing
%I Association for Computing Machinery
%A Po-Hsuan Huang
%A Chia-Heng Tu
%A Shen-Ming Chung
%D 2021
%P 491–500
%K deep neural network compilation, deep neural networks, privacy-preserving inference, secure two-party computation
%C Virtual Event, Republic of Korea

%0 Conference Paper
%T Two-level data sharing method based on classification and grading watermark identification
%@ 9798400718250
%U https://doi.org/10.1145/3689218.3689232
%R 10.1145/3689218.3689232
%X Aiming at the problem of difficult flow tracing and high data confidentiality requirements in the two-level metering data sharing of "headquarters-province side", a watermark identification method based on energy metering and carbon metering data classification and grading is constructed. First, when the provincial data is uploaded to the headquarters, the metering data embedded with the identity ID information of each province is uploaded, and the headquarters can extract the watermark traceability after receiving it. Subsequently, at the stage of data distribution at the headquarters side, the correlation between data attributes and business is calculated based on information gain and Gini coefficient, and the order of correlation index is determined, so as to determine the classification strategy for data privacy based on the correlation. Finally, the headquarters side uses a watermark embedding algorithm to encrypt the data of each partition and then distribute it, the provincial side applies for different partition keys according to business needs, extracts and decrypts the watermarks of the distributed data, and thus realizes the permission management of access data. Experimental results show that the proposed method can accurately trace the transmission direction of metering data, meet the coordinated and efficient processing of two-level metering data transmission and application, solve the information redundancy problem of transmitted data, effectively improve data security and applicability, and provide strong technical support for energy metering and carbon metering data transmission sharing.
%B Proceedings of the 2024 6th International Conference on Pattern Recognition and Intelligent Systems
%I Association for Computing Machinery
%A Jian Dou
%A Xuan Liu
%A Huaiying Shang
%A Shuang Qie
%D 2024
%P 28–34
%K carbon metering data, collaborative processing, data classification and grading, data traceability, energy metering data, watermark embedding
%C Hong Kong, Hong Kong

%0 Proceeding
%T Proceedings of the 16th ACM SIGPLAN International Conference on Software Language Engineering
%@ 9798400703966
%X Welcome to the 16th ACM SIGPLAN International Conference on Software Language Engineering (SLE) held in October 2023 as part of SPLASH 2023. Software Language Engineering (SLE) is a thriving research discipline targeted at establishing an engineering approach to the development, use, and maintenance of software languages, that is, of languages for the specification, modeling and tooling of software. Key topics of interest for SLE include approaches, methodologies and tools for language design and implementation with a focus on techniques for static and behavioral semantics, generative or interpretative approaches (including transformation languages and code generation) as well as meta-languages and tools (including language workbenches). Techniques enabling the testing, simulation or formal verification for language validation purposes are also of particular interest. SLE also accommodates empirical evaluation and experience reports of language engineering tools, such as user studies evaluating usability, performance benchmarks or industrial applications.
%I Association for Computing Machinery
%D 2023
%C Cascais, Portugal

