%0 Conference Paper
%T Research on Simulation Modeling Methods for the Multi-process Coal Supply Chain under Rail-sea Intermodal Transport
%@ 9798400723339
%U https://doi.org/10.1145/3832928.3832962
%R 10.1145/3832928.3832962
%X We focuse on the complexity and multi-process coordination challenges within the coal supply chain under rail-sea intermodal transportation, presenting a multi-agent system (MAS)-based simulation modeling framework. Through analysis of dynamic interdependencies among core processes—including rail transport, port transshipment, maritime shipping, and power plant unloading—a hybrid simulation model is developed. This model synergistically integrates discrete-event simulation (DES) and system dynamics (SD) methodologies to emulate the coal supply chain. It supports the simulation of different train scheduling plans and vessel allocation strategies, thereby facilitating data-driven decision-making for optimized coal logistics. The experimental results show that the model's fidelity in replicating rail-sea intermodal operations, achieving simulation accuracy within 5% error margins, which provide effective support for scheduling decisions.
