%0 Journal Article
%T Trace-Guided Synthesis of Effectful Test Generators
%V 10
%U https://doi.org/10.1145/3808264
%R 10.1145/3808264
%X Several recently proposed program logics have incorporated notions of under approximation into their design, enabling them to reason about reachability rather than safety. In this paper, we explore how similar ideas can be integrated into an expressive type and effect system. We use the resulting underapproximate type specifications to guide the synthesis of test generators that probe the behavior of effectful black-box systems. A key novelty of our type language is its ability to capture underapproximate behaviors of effectful operations using symbolic traces that expose latent data and control dependencies, constraints that must be preserved by the test sequences the generator outputs. We implement this approach in a tool called Clouseau, and evaluate it on a diverse range of applications by integrating Clouseau’s synthesized generators into property-based testing frameworks like QCheck and model-checking tools like P. In both settings, the generators synthesized by Clouseau are significantly more effective than the default testing strategy, and are competitive with state-of-the-art, handwritten solutions.
%N PLDI
%J Proc. ACM Program. Lang.
%I Association for Computing Machinery
%A Zhe Zhou
%A Ankush Desai
%A Benjamin Delaware
%A Suresh Jagannathan
%D 2026
%P Article 186
%K property-based testing, synthesis, type systems, underapproximation

%0 Conference Paper
%T QuantumCode2Vec: Towards Learned Representations for Quantum Software Engineering Tasks
%@ 9798400726361
%U https://doi.org/10.1145/3803437.3805593
%R 10.1145/3803437.3805593
%X Classical software engineering has been shaped by learned code representations, where approaches such as code2vec, CodeBERT, and recent large-scale code models support tasks including clone detection, structural anomaly detection, and code search. Quantum software engineering, however continues to build primarily on syntactic and rule-based techniques. Existing representations and tools such as QChecker, NiCad for quantum programs, and LintQ apply pattern matching and static analysis but they do not capture program-level semantics across circuits. Essentially, these representations are not designed for Aritificial Intelligence and Large Language Model pipelines for quantum software engineering. We present QuantumCode2Vec, a learned representation of quantum programs that captures structural patterns at the level of algorithmic behavior. Driven by the impact of code2vec, the approach decomposes quantum circuits into quantum-aware paths that reflect qubit evolution, entanglement structure, and circuit properties, and aggregates them through attention into fixed length representations and embeddings. Across canonical quantum algorithms, the learned representations group circuits by algorithm, remain stable under superficial changes, and generalize across circuit sizes. In a retrieval setting, similar circuits consistently correspond to the same algorithm, supporting semantic clone detection and code search. In this paper, our goal is to position QuantumCode2Vec as a fundamental learned representation and emedding for quantum software engineering.
%B Proceedings of the 34th ACM International Conference on the Foundations of Software Engineering
%I Association for Computing Machinery
%A Venkat Kaushal Thippisetty
%A Sridhar Chimalakonda
%D 2026
%P 1386–1390
%K quantum software engineering, code embeddings, representation learning, quantum programs, clone detection, code search
