%0 Conference Paper
%T QuSim-Join: Provably Optimal Quadratic Speedup in Set Similarity Joins via Quantum Amplitude Estimation
%@ 9798400727030
%U https://doi.org/10.1145/3811628.3811832
%R 10.1145/3811628.3811832
%X Set similarity join (SSJ)—finding all record pairs with Jaccard similarity exceeding threshold&nbsp;θ —is foundational to data integration and entity resolution. Classical filter–verify algorithms are information-theoretically bottlenecked at Θ(1/ε2) MinHash evaluations per candidate pair; at tight precision (θ = 0.9, ε = 0.01), this demands 10, 000 hash evaluations per pair.We present QuSim-Join, the first hybrid classical–quantum algorithm for SSJ. Our central contribution is the Jaccard encoding operator&nbsp;(mathcal {A}_{mathrm{enc}}), a unitary on (⌈log 2N⌉ + 1) qubits whose flag-qubit amplitude equals (sqrt {J(A,B)}) exactly, reducing Jaccard estimation to quantum amplitude estimation (QAE). By the Brassard–Høyer–Mosca–Tapp framework&nbsp;[6], this yields ε -accurate estimates in (mathcal {O}(1/varepsilon)) queries—a provably optimal quadratic improvement over the classical Ω(1/ε2) lower bound&nbsp;[29]. The full pipeline achieves (mathcal {O}!bigl ((C/varepsilon)log (C/delta)bigr)) total query complexity with a global union-bound correctness certificate over all&nbsp;C candidates. We provide matching lower bounds, a complete noise characterisation under depolarising error, and a quantum-walk extension for candidate generation conjectured to achieve (mathcal {O}!bigl (n^{2/3}cdot operatorname{poly}(1/varepsilon , log n,log N)bigr)) end-to-end under QRAM.Experiments on five benchmarks (DBLP, Amazon, WDC product corpus, synthetic near-duplicates, biomedical entities) validate all complexity predictions. At θ = 0.9, ε = 0.01, QuSim-Join reduces oracle evaluations per pair from 10, 000 to 113 (88.5 ×), with end-to-end per-pair throughput gains up to 102 × on large corpora, and quantum advantage confirmed on IBM Quantum hardware up to circuit depth ≈ 18 (p ≈ 10− 3).
%B Proceedings of the 3rd workshop on Quantum Computing and Quantum-Inspired Technology for Data-Intensive Systems and Applications
%I Association for Computing Machinery
%A Prateek P Kulkarni
%A Aakarsh Alam
%D 2026
%P 10–22
%K Set similarity join, Quantum amplitude estimation, MinHash, Locality-sensitive hashing, Entity resolution
%C 


%0 Conference Paper
%T A Fine-Grained and Efficient Reliability Analysis Framework for Noisy Quantum Circuits
%@ 9798400725708
%U https://doi.org/10.1145/3801489.3806854
%R 10.1145/3801489.3806854
%X Evaluating the reliability of noisy quantum circuits is essential for implementing quantum algorithms on noisy quantum devices. While state fidelity is the most faithful indicator of circuit reliability, it is experimentally and computationally prohibitive to obtain. Alternative metrics, although easier to compute, often fail to accurately reflect circuit reliability. We propose a fine-grained, scalable, and interpretable framework for efficient reliability evaluation of noisy quantum circuits. We introduce the Noise Proxy Circuit (NPC), which removes all logical operations while preserving the complete sequence of noise channels, thereby providing an abstraction of cumulative noise effects. Based on the NPC, we define Proxy Fidelity, a reliability metric that quantifies both qubit-level and circuit-level reliability. We further develop an analytical algorithm to estimate Proxy Fidelity under depolarizing, thermal relaxation, and SPAM error channels. The proposed framework achieves fidelity-level reliability estimation while remaining execution-free, scalable, and interpretable. Experimental results on real and simulated IBM quantum devices show that our method accurately estimates circuit fidelity, with an average absolute difference (AAD) ranging from 0.031 to 0.069 across diverse circuits and devices.
%B Abstracts of the 2026 ACM SIGMETRICS International Conference on Measurement and Modeling of Computer Systems
%I Association for Computing Machinery
%A Jindi Wu
%A Tianjie Hu
%A Qun Li
%D 2026
%P 168–170
%K noisy quantum computing, reliability evaluation, quantum noise modeling
%C USA

%0 Conference Paper
%T ParserHawk: Hardware-aware parser generator using program synthesis
%@ 9798400715242
%U https://doi.org/10.1145/3718958.3750484
%R 10.1145/3718958.3750484
%X Parser programs are becoming increasingly complex to accommodate intricate network packet formats and advanced protocols. Existing parser compilers incorporate predefined program rewrite rules to output the low-level parser implementation. Yet, these rules are often brittle and sensitive to how the input parser program is written. As a result, generated implementations could consume more hardware resources than necessary. In some cases, these compilers unnecessarily reject valid parser programs that could have fit within the target device parser's resource constraints.We leverage program-synthesis-based techniques to build a parser compiler, ParserHawk, for 2 network devices: the Intel Infrastructure Processing Unit (IPU) and the Barefoot Tofino programmable switch. Naively formulating code generation as a program synthesis problem can take hours, if not days, to complete. As a result, ParserHawk incorporates several optimization algorithms, which achieve a geometric mean speed-up of 309.44×. Within a compile time on the order of minutes for most benchmarks, ParserHawk can correctly compile parser programs rejected by existing compilers and can generate parser implementations that use fewer hardware resources.
%B Proceedings of the ACM SIGCOMM 2025 Conference
