%0 Proceeding
%T Proceedings of the 2025 Workshop on Research on Offensive and Defensive Techniques in the Context of Man At The End (MATE) Attacks
%@ 9798400719066
%X It is our great pleasure to welcome you to CheckMATE Research on offensive and defensive techniques in the context of Man At The End (MATE) attacks, held as part of the ACM Conference on Computer and Communications Security (CCS 2025). CheckMATE continues to foster collaboration and dialogue on the most cutting-edge research in a critical area of security.The focus of CheckMATE is on the Man-At-The-End (MATE) attacker model, where adversaries gain access to avictim's software and/or hardware environment to observe, modify, and extract sensitive information. This caninclude cryptographic keys or configuration data, and the goals of such attacks may range from compromising code integrity to inserting backdoors. Typical attack scenarios involve stolen devices like smartphones or tampering with software that protects premium content and services.This year's workshop emphasizes both new models and techniques to defend against software tampering, reverse engineering, and piracy, as well as innovative attack strategies that reveal the need for more robust defenses.
%I Association for Computing Machinery
%E Sébastien Bardin
%E Michele Ianni
%E Hyungon Moon
