%0 Conference Paper
%T Leveraging LLMs for Automated Translation of Legacy Code: A Case Study on PL/SQL to Java Transformation
%@ 9798400713859
%U https://doi.org/10.1145/3756681.3757007
%R 10.1145/3756681.3757007
%X The VT legacy system, comprising approximately 2.5 million lines of PL/SQL code, lacks consistent documentation and automated tests, posing significant challenges for refactoring and modernisation. This study investigates the feasibility of leveraging large language models (LLMs) to assist in translating PL/SQL code into Java for the modernised "VTF3" system. By leveraging a dataset comprising 10 PL/SQL-to-Java code pairs and 15 Java classes, which collectively established a domain model for the translated files, multiple LLMs were evaluated. Furthermore, we propose a customized prompting strategy that integrates chain-of-guidance reasoning with n-shot prompting. Our findings indicate that this methodology effectively guides LLMs in generating syntactically accurate translations while also achieving functional correctness. However, the findings are limited by the small sample size of available code files and the restricted access to test cases used for validating the correctness of the generated code. Nevertheless, these findings lay the groundwork for scalable, automated solutions in modernising large legacy systems.
%B Proceedings of the 29th International Conference on Evaluation and Assessment in Software Engineering
%I Association for Computing Machinery
%A Lola Solovyeva
%A Eduardo Carneiro Oliveira
%A Shiyu Fan
%A Alper Tuncay
%A Shamil Gareev
%A Andrea Capiluppi
%D 2025
%P 809–813
%K Large-language models, Legacy code, Code modernisation, Automated code translation, PL/SQL
%C 


%0 Conference Paper
%T Porting ThunderKittens from CUDA to SYCL for Intel GPU: Process, Challenges, and Lessons Learned
%@ 9798400724992
%U https://doi.org/10.1145/3811257.3811269
%R 10.1145/3811257.3811269
%X This paper presents our experience porting ThunderKittens, a CUDA-based GPU programming framework for deep learning kernels, to SYCL for Intel GPUs. We follow a three-stage process: automated conversion, manual adaptation, and architecture-specific optimization. The main task is translating ThunderKittens’ C++ template library and replacing PTX-based implementations with alternatives for Intel GPUs. This required changes to both register layout and memory movement, replacing CUDA’s shared-memory-centric execution path with register layouts better suited to Intel GPUs and L1-prefetch-based latency hiding. Our results show where current CUDA-to-SYCL translation tools help and where manual porting is still required. Although full performance portability remains difficult across architectures, the same high-level abstractions can be preserved across platforms: our SYCL port comes within 5% of Intel’s hand-optimized oneDNN GEMM performance and exceeds it on the fused kernels we evaluated. These results show what is required to port framework-level GPU abstractions across architectures.
%B Proceedings of the International Workshop on OpenCL and SYCL
%I Association for Computing Machinery
%A Yehong Jiang
%A Shen Chen
%A Fangwen Fu
%A Vincent Lu
%A Yen-Kuang Chen
%A Hong Wang
%A Xinmin Tian
%D 2026
%P Article 9
%K CUDA to SYCL porting, Cross-platform GPU programming, Performance portability, ThunderKittens, Intel GPU optimization, SYCLomatic
%C 


