%0 Conference Paper
%T QuaMap: A Multi-Backend Benchmark Dataset for Quantum Circuit Mapping and Learning-Based Compiler Evaluation
%@ 9798400722585
%U https://doi.org/10.1145/3770854.3785697
%R 10.1145/3770854.3785697
%X Mapping quantum circuits to specific hardware backends is a crucial step in quantum program compilation, yet current benchmarks rarely capture the backend-aware effects of this process. We present QuaMap, the first large-scale, open benchmark dataset that systematically links 75 representative quantum algorithms with over 210,000 hardware-aware transpiled circuits on 10 IBM Quantum devices. Unlike existing circuit collections, QuaMap explicitly records the impact of topology-constrained compilation by providing both the original and transpiled circuits together with rich structural and performance metrics (circuit depth, gate counts with various qubits, and CNOT count). QuaMap defines four benchmark tasks, involving circuit embedding classification, transpilation metric prediction, mapping quality ranking, and transferability evaluation, and includes reproducible baselines for each task, showing that graph neural networks substantially outperform heuristic and classical models. By making the data, code, and evaluation protocols openly available, QuaMap establishes a standardized foundation for data-driven research on quantum compilation, enabling the community to analyze layout effects, design predictive models, and compare mapping strategies across devices.
%B Proceedings of the 32nd ACM SIGKDD Conference on Knowledge Discovery and Data Mining V.1
%I Association for Computing Machinery
%A Ziming Zhao
%A Tingting Li
%A Jianwei Yin
%D 2026
%P 2913–2923
%K quantum computing, circuit transpilation, benchmark and dataset
%C Republic of Korea

