%0 Conference Paper
%T C-3PQ: A Closeness Centrality-based Circuit Partitioner for Quantum Simulations
%@ 9798400725227
%U https://doi.org/10.1145/3797905.3807864
%R 10.1145/3797905.3807864
%X Simulating quantum circuits (QC) on high-performance computing (HPC) systems is essential for validating quantum algorithms and probing the potential of large-scale quantum computation before the advent of scalable quantum hardware. However, state vector simulations are resource-intensive, often requiring large clusters with thousands of compute nodes and large amounts of memory. To address this, we introduce C-3PQ, an end-to-end framework that minimizes inter-node data movement through an efficient circuit partitioning scheme alongside a flexible code generator to offer a portable simulation solution. By formulating the distribution of the state vector and circuit over multiple nodes as a graph problem, we utilize closeness centrality to assess gate importance and design a fast, scalable partitioning method. C-3PQ compiles the resulting partitions into highly optimized kernels targeting a wide range of hardware platforms from Intel, AMD, NVIDIA and ARM. Moreover, compared to a state-of-the-art implementation specific for NVIDIA platforms, C-3PQ achieves a speedup of up to (40%) at a fraction of the cost when partitioning the state vector.
%B Proceedings of the 40th ACM International Conference on Supercomputing
