%J SIGMOD Rec.
%I Association for Computing Machinery
%A Mateusz Gienieczko
%A Maximilian Kuschewski
%A Thomas Neumann
%A Viktor Leis
%A Jana Giceva
%D 2026
%P 62–72

%0 Conference Paper
%T Illustrating TinyML on Microcontrollers with an Acoustic Monitoring Project
%@ 9798400720628
%U https://doi.org/10.1145/3746467.3801538
%R 10.1145/3746467.3801538
%X We present an end-to-end project designed to illustrate the development of machine learning solutions for resource-constrained microcontroller units to students. The project focuses on real-time acoustic monitoring of household appliances, specifically a dishwasher, using an Arduino Nano RP2040 Connect equipped with an ARM Cortex-M0+ processor and 264 KB of SRAM. The implementation integrates machine learning, digital signal processing, and embedded systems by utilizing a C-based prediction pipeline that performs a Fast Fourier Transform of audio samples, feature scaling, and prediction with a logistic regression model. Experimental results show that the model achieves a balanced accuracy of 95.6% in offline testing and maintains a respectable 92.8% accuracy when deployed for online monitoring. By providing an open-source solution using low-cost, accessible hardware, this project offers a practical framework for teaching the complete lifecycle of developing and deploying ML models in embedded systems.
%B Proceedings of the 2026 ACM Southeast Conference
%I Association for Computing Machinery
%A Ronald James Nowling
%D 2026
%P 281–286
%K acoustic monitoring, machine learning, embedded systems, digital signal processing, pedagogy, microcontrollers
