%0 Conference Paper
%T Furina: A Light-weight WebAssembly Runtime for ICS
%@ 9798400719110
%U https://doi.org/10.1145/3733823.3764514
%R 10.1145/3733823.3764514
%X Industrial Control Systems (ICS) face inherent limitations in application management, security isolation, and dynamic upgrade capabilities, increasingly necessitating edge-optimized system architectures. Microservices offer an attractive paradigm where users can execute small applications without dealing with operational issues such as system configuration and resource management. However, existing container and virtual machine based implementations are too heavy-weight for a resource-constrained ICS. Furthermore, ICS workloads that focus on short-run computations exhibit mismatches with existing general-purpose computing systems. WebAssembly, with its lightweight sandboxing capabilities, emerges as a viable approach to support ICS. In this paper, we introduce Furina, a novel WebAssembly-based microservice framework designed for ICS that enables fast function startup, convenient management, and dynamic upgrades, coupled with a high-performance and secure data plane. To establish a lightweight service mesh, Furina employs WebAssembly-based sidecars instead of individual userspace sidecars. We leverage a lightweight function isolation model implemented by our WebAssembly-based software fault isolation infrastructure to ensure secure access to data. These lightweight sandboxes are designed to support high-density computing: with fast startup and teardown. An evaluation conducted with varying workloads demonstrate the effectiveness of the microservice runtime designed for ICS.
%B Proceedings of the 2025 Workshop on Re-design Industrial Control Systems with Security
%I Association for Computing Machinery
%A Hanwen Lei
%A Ding Li
%A Yao Guo
%D 2025
%P 1–6
%K Industrial control system; WebAssembly; Microservice; Sidecar
%C 


%0 Journal Article
%T Syntactic Implicit Parameters with Static Overloading
%V 10
%U https://doi.org/10.1145/3808336
%R 10.1145/3808336
%X Implicits provide a powerful mechanism for term-based inference, where “obvious” arguments can be omitted and inferred by the type checker. This can greatly reduce the programmer’s burden and improve the clarity of expression. As such, many languages support a form of implicits in practice, such as type classes in Haskell or Lean, or implicits in Scala. Unfortunately, many of these systems have become increasingly complex and often require significant implementation effort.In this paper we take a fresh look at the design space with an arguably simpler approach based on two orthogonal features: syntactic implicit parameters and static overloading. Each of these features is limited in scope and has a straightforward implementation. Taken together though, they are surprisingly expressive and we believe they can cover many of the common usage scenarios of implicits in practice.We formalize our system and provide various examples, and prove our elaboration is coherent. We also give an inference algorithm and show it is sound and complete. Our system is fully implemented in the Koka language, and we describe our experience with these features at scale, and discuss further extensions.
%N PLDI
%J Proc. ACM Program. Lang.
%I Association for Computing Machinery
%A Daan Leijen
%A Tim Whiting
%D 2026
%P Article 258
%K Implicit Parameters, Static Overloading, Local Qualification

