%T Knit Everything: Surfaces, Systems, and the Future of Textiles
%@ 9798400725418
%U https://doi.org/10.1145/3799818.3812111
%R 10.1145/3799818.3812111
%X Industrial knitting sits at an unusual intersection of craft, manufacturing, and computation. Despite decades of CNC control, the prevailing design model for machine knitting remains anchored in a flat, bitmap-based paradigm—slow to iterate, spatially limited, and inaccessible to designers without specialized training. LOOP, the computational knit design system developed within VARIANT3D, proposes a new paradigm grounded in 3D surface geometry, discrete differential geometry, and a philosophy of software-as-material. This paper describes the LOOP system: its data model, pipeline, visualization stack, and underlying design philosophy—including its treatment of fabrication constraints as design dialogue, its role as a living tech pack for distributed manufacturing, and its position within a broader socio-cultural ecology of making.
