%T QUBO Model for Energy Planning: Quantum-Enhanced Microgrid Optimization for Rural Electrification in Africa
%@ 9798400724886
%U https://doi.org/10.1145/3795101.3814703
%R 10.1145/3795101.3814703
%X Rural electrification planning in developing regions in Africa constitutes a large-scale multi-objective combinatorial optimization problem, involving competing objectives such as cost minimization, population coverage maximization, and energy capacity allocation. The discrete, high-dimensional nature of this problem challenges classical optimization methods, which often rely on heuristics and struggle to explore complex search spaces efficiently. In this work, we introduce Q-Energic, a quantum-enhanced framework that formulates microgrid planning as a Quadratic Unconstrained Binary Optimization (QUBO) problem. The model encodes site selection and demand coverage within a unified objective, with operational constraints incorporated through penalty terms. We evaluate the framework on a real-world dataset of 50 candidate sites from Ethiopia's Global Electrification Platform, and benchmark across quantum-inspired annealing, gate-based optimization using QAOA, and classical methods including greedy search, simulated annealing, and tabu search. Results reveal consistent trade-offs across solvers: classical methods provide strong cost efficiency and feasibility, while quantum-inspired approaches improve exploration and achieve higher coverage in some configurations. QAOA produces feasible but lower-quality solutions under current hardware constraints. The framework facilitates systematic benchmarking across heterogeneous solvers and provides quantitative insights into the performance and limitations of quantum optimization in data-driven energy planning, laying the groundwork for scalable, constraint-aware electrification model design.
%B Proceedings of the Genetic and Evolutionary Computation Conference Companion
%I Association for Computing Machinery
%A Yousra Farhani
%A Helarie Rose Medie Fah
%A Ahmed Samir
%A Marzuq Yussif Etsie Adam
%A Kenedy Mwendwa Mulila
%A Abdulmajid Osumanu
%D 2026
%P 1550–1558
%K quantum optimization, QUBO formulation, microgrid planning, quantum annealing, QAOA, AutoQUBO, combinatorial optimization, energy systems optimization
%C Centro Internacional de Convenciones CIC-ANDE, San Jose, Costa Rica

%0 Journal Article
%T Comparative Benchmarking of Utility-Scale Quantum Emulators
%V 7
%U https://doi.org/10.1145/3776567
%R 10.1145/3776567
%X Evaluating quantum algorithms at utility-scale—involving more than 100 qubits—is a key step toward advancing real-world applications of quantum computing. In this study, we benchmark seven state-of-the-art quantum emulators employing techniques such as tensor networks, matrix product states (MPS), decision diagrams, and factorized ket based methods, running on CPU based hardware and focusing on effectively exact simulations. Performance is assessed on 13 benchmark circuits from the MQTBench library, spanning circuit sizes from 4 to 1,024 qubits. Our results reveal that MPS-based emulators outperform other approaches overall, successfully solving 8 benchmarks up to the maximum size of 1,024 qubits and 12 benchmarks up to at least 100 qubits in less than 5 minutes. We find evidence that all circuits except a random one can be simulated in polynomial time. This work demonstrates that quantum emulators can faithfully simulate a broad range of large and complex universal quantum circuits with high fidelity, far beyond the limits of statevector simulators and today’s quantum hardware.
%N 2
%J ACM Transactions on Quantum Computing
%I Association for Computing Machinery
%A Anna Leonteva
%A Guido Masella
%A Maxime Outteryck
%A Asier Peiro Orioli
%A Shannon Whitlock
%D 2026
%P Article 8
%K Quantum emulator, quantum circuit simulation, quantum utility, benchmarking, matrix product states, tensor networks, optimization

%0 Conference Paper
%T Tetris: A Compilation Framework for VQA Applications in Quantum Computing
%@ 9798350326581
%U https://doi.org/10.1109/ISCA59077.2024.00029
%R 10.1109/isca59077.2024.00029
%X Quantum computing has shown promise in solving complex problems by leveraging the principles of superposition and entanglement. Variational quantum algorithms (VQA) are a class of algorithms suited for near-term quantum computers due to their modest requirements of qubits and depths of computation. This paper introduces Tetris - a compilation framework for VQA applications on near-term quantum devices. Tetris focuses on reducing two-qubit gates in the compilation process since a two-qubit gate has an order of magnitude more significant error and execution time than a single-qubit gate. Tetris exploits unique opportunities in the circuit synthesis stage often overlooked by the state-of-the-art VQA compilers for reducing the number of two-qubit gates. Tetris comes with a refined IR of Pauli string to express such a two-qubit gate optimization opportunity. Moreover, Tetris is equipped with a fast bridging approach that mitigates the hardware mapping cost. Overall, Tetris demonstrates a reduction of up to 41.3% in CNOT gate counts, 37.9% in circuit depth, and 42.6% in circuit duration for various molecules of different sizes and structures compared with the state-of-the-art approaches. Tetris is open-sourced at this link.
%B Proceedings of the 51st Annual International Symposium on Computer Architecture
%I IEEE Press
%A Yuwei Jin
%A Zirui Li
%A Fei Hua
%A Tianyi Hao
%A Huiyang Zhou
%A Yipeng Huang
%A Eddy Z. Zhang
%D 2025
%P 277–292
%C Buenos Aires, Argentina

%0 Conference Paper
%T Qonductor: A Cloud Orchestrator for Quantum Computing
%@ 9798400714665
%U https://doi.org/10.1145/3712285.3759785
%R 10.1145/3712285.3759785
%X We describe Qonductor, a cloud orchestrator for hybrid quantum-classical applications that run on heterogeneous hybrid resources. Qonductor abstracts away the complexity of hybrid programming and resource management by exposing the Qonductor API, a high-level and hardware-agnostic API. The resource estimator strategically balances quantum and classical resources to mitigate resource contention and the effects of hardware noise. The hybrid scheduler automates job scheduling on hybrid resources and balances the tradeoff between users’ objectives of QoS and the cloud operator’s objective of resource efficiency.We implement an open-source prototype and evaluate Qonductor using more than 7000 real quantum runs on the IBM quantum cloud to simulate real cloud workloads. Qonductor achieves up to 54% lower job completion times (JCTs) while sacrificing 3% execution quality, balances the load across QPU, which increases quantum resource utilization by up to 66%, and scales with growing system sizes and loads.
%B Proceedings of the International Conference for High Performance Computing, Networking, Storage and Analysis
