%0 Conference Paper
%T A user-oriented macro processor
%@ 9781450374866
%U https://doi.org/10.1145/800186.810640
%R 10.1145/800186.810640
%X Machine-language assemblers map statements in a source language to the machine language of a particular computer. The source language accepted by the assembler is, for the most part, defined in a one-to-one manner with the hardware instructions available on the target computer.The addition of a macro processor to the assembler, referred to then as a macro assembler, effectively extends the range of the source languages accepted by the assembler. This is achieved by providing the user with a means of describing how his particular source-language statements are to be translated into assembler language statements. The description of a translation is called a 'macro definition' and may be input to, and used by, the macro processor during an assembly. The actual range of describable translations is dependent upon the features provided in the macro processor and the macro definition language.
%B Proceedings of the 1968 23rd ACM national conference
%I Association for Computing Machinery
%A D. L. Marrs
%A C. W. Collins
%A H. A. Hess
